window.__registerChapter({"num": 24, "slug": "024_Interstitial_Lung_Diseases_of_Childhood", "title": "Interstitial Lung Diseases of Childhood", "authors": "Jordi Costa-Colomer, Antonio Moreno-Galdo, Alba Torrent-Vernetta, Maria Molina-Molina", "part_num": "IV", "part_title": "Cardiopulmonary Disorders", "word_count": 9373, "pearls": ["Highresolution computed tomography (HRCT) of the chest should be the first-line imaging investigation because it allows for distinguishing broadly between chILD and noninterstitial lung diseases .", "De novo or inherited autosomal dominant pathogenic variants in FOXF1 are responsible for ACDMPV; rare cases have been described associated with homozygous autosomal recessive pathogenic variants in STRA6 .", "Clinically, NEHI is characterized by tachypnea, retractions, crackles, and hypoxemia, which typically appear between 2 and 6 months of age.", "ABCA3 deficiency is autosomal recessively inherited and is the most common cause of surfactant protein deficiency.", "Active areas of investigation include targeted gene therapy and stem cell therapy.", "Dyskeratosis congenita (DC) is an inherited bone marrow failure and cancer predisposition syndrome caused by defects in telomere maintenance and characterized by the clinical triad of abnormal nails, reticular skin pigmentation, and oral leukoplakia.", "The most common presentations are follicular bronchiolitis, lymphoid interstitial pneumonitis, and granulomatous lymphocytic interstitial lung disease.", "Hermansky-Pudlak syndrome (HPS) is a rare multisystem hereditary disorder characterized by oculocutaneous albinism, a bleeding diathesis due to platelet aggregation defects, and other manifestations including neutropenia and granulomatous colitis (Fig. 24.8E–F)."], "genes": ["ABCA3", "ACTA2", "AP3B1", "BRAF", "C2", "C3", "CH50", "CSF2RA", "CSF2RB", "DKC1", "EIF2AK4", "FGF10", "FGFR2", "FLNA", "FOXF1", "GATA2", "GBA1", "JO1"], "omims": [], "subtopics": ["Cardiopulmonary Disorders", "Etiology, Classification, and Identifications", "Diagnosis", "Classification of Childhood Interstitial Lung Diseases", "Diffuse Developmental Disorders", "Pulmonary Interstitial Glycogenosis", "Neuroendocrine Cell Hyperplasia of Infancy", "Surfactant Protein Deficiencies", "Surfactant Protein B Deficiency", "Surfactant Protein C Deficiency", "Surfactant Protein A Deficiency", "ABCA3 Deficiency", "NKX2-1 Deficiency", "Histopathology of Surfactant Protein Deficiencies"], "figs": [{"img": "fig_p0564_02.jpg", "label": "Fig. 24.1", "caption": "Investigations in childhood interstitial lung disease (chILD) diagnostic workup. (A) The investigations are performed one after the other to reach a chILD etiology. The first step is usually done by the general pediatrician (framed in red) , and includes family history, clinical evaluation, chest radiography, and routine laboratory tests. (B) Age at onset is crucial information that helps to consider some etiologies more than others. (C) Bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) eliminates infectious causes of ILD and provides clues to the most likely diagnoses. (D) When thoracic computed tomography (CT) scan, specific blood tests, BAL, and genetic analyses could not assess a diagnosis, lung biopsy is the last step of the diagnostic process, allowing to assess the most likely diagnoses in most cases. AEC2, Type 2 alveolar epithelial cells; DAH, diffuse alveolar hemorrhage; HSP, hypersensitivity pneumonitis; LIP, lymphoid interstitial pneumonia; NEHI, neuroendocrine cell hyperplasia of infancy; NSIP, nonspecific interstitial pneumonia; OB, obliterans bronchiolitis; PAP, pulmonary alveolar proteinosis; PAS, Periodic acid–Schiff."}, {"img": "fig_p0566_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 24.2", "caption": "Selected illustrative thoracic high-resolution computed tomography images in childhood interstitial lung disease. (A) Neuroendocrine cell hyperplasia of infancy: ground-glass opacification typically involving the right middle lobe and lingula; (B) Pulmonary interstitial glycogenosis: nonspecific and nonuniform ground-glass opacification and mosaic attenuation; (C) Surfactant protein C deficiency: diffuse ground-glass opacities with occasional cystic changes; (D) ABCA3 gene variant: ground-glass opacification with septal thickening and cystic changes; (E) Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis: scattered opacification with intralobular and interlobular septal thickening (the “crazy paving” pattern); (F) Hypersensitivity pneumonitis: ground-glass opacification with mosaic attenuation; (G) FARSB pathogenic variant: diffuse opacification with subpleural and parenchymal cystic changes; (H) TMEM173 pathogenic variant STING -associated vasculopathy with onset in infancy (SAVI): diffuse opacification with septal thickening and cystic changes."}, {"img": "fig_p0567_02.jpg", "label": "Fig. 24.3", "caption": "Neuroendocrine cell hyperplasia of infancy, high-resolution chest CT findings. (A) Axial image shows patchy ground-glass opacification involving the right middle lobe (long black arrow) and lingula (short black arrow) . (B) Ground-glass opacities are likewise predominantly central in location (arrows) . (C) Coronal image shows areas of air-trapping (white arrows) ."}, {"img": "fig_p0567_04.jpg", "label": "Fig. 24.4", "caption": "Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis. Axial CT chest scan demonstrating the “crazy paving” pattern, consisting of ground-glass opacification with superimposed intralobular and interlobular septal thickening."}, {"img": "fig_p0568_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 24.5", "caption": "Pulmonary alveolar microlithiasis. High-resolution computed tomographic images of a 31-yearold female patient with pulmonary alveolar microlithiasis. Images obtained at the levels of the superior (A) and inferior (B) lobes with an intermediary window show hyperdense consolidations in both lungs caused by the confluence of calcified nodules."}, {"img": "fig_p0568_03.jpg", "label": "Fig. 24.6", "caption": "Hypersensitivity pneumonitis. Two examples of three-density sign in patients with nonfibrotic hypersensitivity pneumonitis. (A) Inspiratory CT imaging shows patchy ground-glass attenuation in the anterior left upper lobe (blue circle) . Other lobules are of preserved (normal) attenuation (yellow circle) , and there is lobular decreased attenuation in the posterior left lung (red circle) . No signs of fibrosis are present. (B) Expiratory CT imaging accentuates numerous lobules of decreased attenuation representing air-trapping. (C) Inspiratory CT imaging in a different patient shows diffuse ground-glass attenuation (blue circle) . An adjacent lobule is of preserved (normal) attenuation (yellow circle) , and there are multiple lobules with decreased attenuation (red circle) . No signs of fibrosis are present. (D) Expiratory CT imaging accentuates numerous lobules of decreased attenuation representing air-trapping."}, {"img": "fig_p0571_02.jpg", "label": "Fig. 24.7", "caption": "Selected histological images of lung biopsies in childhood interstitial lung disease (I). (A) Pulmonary interstitial glycogenosis: alveolar simplification and septal thickening with increased mesenchymal cells, hematoxylin-eosin; (B) Pulmonary interstitial glycogenosis: detail of septal thickening with increased mesenchymal cells, hematoxylin-eosin; (C) Congenital alveolar dysplasia with glycogenosis: marked widening of the interstitium and obstruction of the preacinar and intraacinar arteries consistent with severe alveolar developmental disorder; (D) Congenital alveolar dysplasia with glycogenosis: detail of interstitial widening with mesenchymal cells staining positive for glycogen; (E) Chronic pneumonitis of infancy: significant interstitial thickening due to proliferation of fibroblastic and lymphomonocytic cells; (F) Chronic pneumonitis of infancy: detail of type II pneumocyte hyperplasia."}, {"img": "fig_p0572_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 24.8", "caption": "Selected additional histological images of lung biopsies in childhood interstitial lung disease (II). (A) Niemann-Pick disease: total occupation of alveolar spaces, pneumocyte hyperplasia, and septal thickening; (B) Niemann-Pick disease: detail of alveolar proteinosis pattern with lipoproteinaceous material in intra-alveolar macrophages; (C) Neuroendocrine cell hyperplasia of infancy: image of isolated neuroendocrine cells in the bronchiolar wall, Bombesin staining; (D) STING-associated vasculopathy with onset in infancy (SAVI): pattern of lymphocytic interstitial pneumonia and follicular bronchiolitis with septal thickening, T and B lymphocytic aggregates in the interstitium and airways; (E and F) Hermansky-Pudlak syndrome: interstitial pneumonia pattern; predominant subpleural fibrotic pattern with microscopic honeycombing, combined with areas of normal lung (E), and the presence of multiple fibroblastic foci in the fibrotic regions (F)."}, {"img": "fig_p0576_13.jpg", "label": "Fig. 24.9", "caption": "Pathogenic variants in the X-linked gene Filamin A (FLNA) in a 9-month-old female who presented with pulmonary hypertension. (A) Axial lung window CT image shows bilateral multilobar hyperinflation. (B) Coronal brain MR image demonstrates nodular periventricular gray matter heterotopia."}, {"img": "fig_p0578_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 24.10", "caption": "Chest radiograph of a 25-year-old male patient with pulmonary alveolar microlithiasis, showing diffuse opacification in both lungs with a dense micronodular (“sandstorm”) appearance."}, {"img": "fig_p0579_07.jpg", "label": "Fig. 24.11", "caption": "Scadding’s radiographic stages of sarcoidosis with CT correlation . (A and B) “Stage 1.” A 30-year-old man with sarcoidosis: (A) chest radiograph obtained during routine evaluation shows bilateral hilar, bilateral paratracheal, and aorticopulmonary window adenopathy (arrows) , but no parenchymal findings and (B) CT chest shows bilateral hilar (arrows) and subcarinal (asterisk) adenopathy. (C and D) “Stage 2.” A 35-year-old man with sarcoidosis. (C) Chest radiograph shows upper lobe– predominant reticulonodular opacities and bilateral hilar and mediastinal lymphadenopathy. (D) Chest CT shows reticular opacities, ground-glass opacity, and mild architectural distortion in the upper lobes. Perilymphatic nodules cluster along the right major fissure and along peripheral septal lymphatics (arrows) ."}], "tables_meta": [{"label": "TABLE 24.1", "title": "###### 24.1 ##### The Pediatric Interstitial Lung Disease in Children Under 2 Years of Age"}, {"label": "Table 24.2", "title": "SPDS Clinical Features Age and Onset"}, {"label": "Table 24.3", "title": "Elementary Lesions Distribution Suspected Diagnoses"}, {"label": "TABLE 24.4", "title": "##### Investigations in Childhood Interstitial Lung Disease"}, {"label": "Table 24.5", "title": "Investigation Indicates"}, {"label": "Table 24.6", "title": "Gene Inheritance Pattern Phenotypes"}, {"label": "TABLE 24.7", "title": "Telomer e Disorders : Associated Ge nes, Inheritance Patterns, and Clinical Features"}], "body_md": "\n<!-- p.558 -->\n\n## SECTION **IV** \n\n### Cardiopulmonary Disorders \n\n24. Interstitial Lung Diseases of Childhood, 539 \n\n25. Primary Ciliary Dyskinesia, 561 \n\n26. Pulmonary Alveolar Proteinosis, 577 \n\n27. Respiratory and Autonomic Disorders of Infancy, Childhood, and Adulthood (RADICA): Congenital Central Hypoventilation Syndrome (CCHS) and Rapid-Onset Obesity With Hypothalamic Dysfunction, Hypoventilation, and Autonomic Dysregulation (ROHHAD), 589 \n\n28. Pediatric Cardiomyopathies, 609 \n\n29. Congenital/Familial Arrhythmia Syndromes, 633 \n\n30. Vascular and Lymphatic Malformations, 655\n\n<!-- p.559 -->\n\n\n\n<!-- p.560 -->\n\n# 24 Interstitial Lung Diseases of Childhood \n\n**JORDI COSTA-COLOMER, ANTONIO MORENO-GALDÓ, ALBA TORRENT-VERNETTA, MARIA MOLINA-MOLINA** \n\n**Childhood interstitial lung diseases (chILDs)** , also known as diffuse parenchymal lung diseases, are a heterogeneous group of rare lung disorders that mainly affect infants and children and that are characterized by inflammation, fibrosis, or other diffuse histological alterations of the lung parenchyma that lead to lung function deterioration and progressive respiratory failure. The initial symptoms of cough, dyspnea, and recurrent respiratory infections mimic other more common pediatric respiratory conditions, making recognition challenging. Mean incidence is estimated at roughly 8 new cases per million per year and prevalence is estimated at up to 47 cases per million, though true rates are difficult to establish due to the lack of a standardized classification system of ILD in children. \n\nThe chILD disorders can vary widely with respect to underlying causes, clinical presentations, and outcomes, though common features include diffuse abnormalities on chest imaging, as well as abnormal pulmonary functional tests. Specific diagnosis involves a combination of clinical evaluation, imaging studies (e.g., chest radiographs and high-resolution CT scans), lung function tests, genetic tests, and sometimes lung biopsies. While diffuse developmental disorders, pulmonary growth abnormalities, specific diseases of infancy, and surfactant disorders are more frequent in children under 2 years of age, other ILDs such as hypersensitivity pneumonitis, systemic and syndromic diseases, and disorders of the immunocompromised host are more prevalent between 2 and 12 years. Despite a thorough investigation, a cause is not found in up to 12% of cases. \n\nManagement of chILD depends on the specific underlying condition and its severity. Early and appropriate interventions are crucial to prevent progression of lung damage. Treatment may include supportive care, supplemental oxygen, medications to ameliorate inflammation or fibrosis, and in some severe cases lung transplantation. Because of the complexity and rarity of these disorders, a multidisciplinary team of pediatric pulmonologists, radiologists, lung interventionists, pathologists, and geneticists is often involved in establishing an accurate diagnosis and appropriately tailored treatment plan. \n\n#### **Etiology, Classification, and Identifications** \n\nThe precise etiology of chILD is often unknown; specific pathogenic causes include developmental, genetic, and metabolic disorders, autoimmune or inflammatory diseases, and reactive lung responses such as hypersensitivity pneumonitis, post-viral alveolar damage, and medication toxicity. Classification systems based on etiology, age of disease onset, and the immunocompetency of the affected individual have been proposed (Tables 24.1 and 24.2). \n\nThe severity of ILD onset in children is highly variable, ranging from mild nonspecific symptoms, which may delay diagnosis, to a severe presentation, which is more frequent in newborns and infants. Despite the variability in age of onset, different pathogenic genetic variants, other inciting causes, and disease course, the respiratory symptoms are similar among the different chILDs; some distinctions in presenting symptoms and the differential diagnosis based on age of onset are worth mentioning. \n\n**Newborns:** chILD may present shortly after birth with unexplained respiratory distress and hypoxia in a _term_ neonate. Breathing difficulties may rapidly escalate to requiring intubation and mechanical ventilation, with relentlessly progressive respiratory failure leading to lung transplantation or death. The usual causes are pathogenic variants in surfactant proteins and diffuse developmental disorders; some individuals with surfactant protein-related disorders may partially recover to a state of chronic respiratory failure (Table 24.3) (see Chapter 26). The same disorders can affect preterm neonates, though respiratory distress in this cohort is more often directly related to prematurity and the yield of diagnostic testing for chILD in premature neonates is typically lower. Nonetheless, testing should still be considered, particularly in preterm neonates who fail to respond to treatment as expected or in whom symptoms are especially severe. \n\n**Infants (0–2 years):** common symptoms in infants with chILD include tachypnea, failure to thrive, and dry cough with or without wheezing in the absence of an active respiratory tract infection. Signs include hypoxia and crackles, though auscultation can be normal in 30%\n\n<!-- p.561 -->\n\n###### **TABLE** \n\n###### **24.1** \n\n##### **The Pediatric Interstitial Lung Disease in Children Under 2 Years of Age** \n\n|**Age-Related Interstitial Lung Diseases in Infancy**<br>**and Early Childhood**|**Idiopathic Interstitial Lung Diseases**<br>Desquamative interstitial pneumonitis|\n|---|---|\n|Diffuse developmental disorders<br>Acinar dysplasia<br>Congenital alveolar dysplasia<br>Alveolar capillary dysplasia with misalignment of pulmonary<br>veins (some caused by_FOXF1_pathogenic variants)<br>Growth abnormalities reflecting deficient alveolarization<br>Pulmonary hypoplasia<br>Chronic neonatal lung disease<br>Chromosomal disorders<br>|Lymphocytic interstitial pneumonitis and related disorders<br>Nonspecific interstitial pneumonitis (cellular/fibrotic)<br>Eosinophilic pneumonia<br>Bronchiolitis obliterans syndrome<br>Pulmonary hemosiderosis and acute idiopathic pulmonary<br>hemorrhage of infancy<br>Pulmonary vascular disorders<br>Pulmonary lymphatic disorders<br>Pulmonary microlithiasis|\n|Congenital heart disease<br>Neuroendocrine cell hyperplasia of infancy|**Systemic Disorders With Pulmonary Manifestations**|\n|<br>Pulmonary interstitial glycogenosis (infantile cellular<br>interstitial pneumonia)<br>Surfactant dysfunction disorders (pulmonary alveolar<br>proteinosis)<br>Surfactant protein B variant<br>Surfactant protein C variant<br>_ABCA3_variant<br>Granulocyte-macrophage colony-stimulating factor<br>receptor_(CSF2RA)_variant<br>_NKX2.1_(transcription factor for SP-B, SPC, ABCA3)|Anti-GBM disease<br>Granulomatosis with polyangiitis<br>Microscopic polyangiitis<br>Idiopathic pulmonary capillaritis<br>Gaucher disease and other storage diseases<br>Malignant infiltrates<br>Hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis<br>MAS<br>Langerhans cell histiocytosis<br>Sarcoidosis<br>Systemic sclerosis|\n|**Interstitial Lung Disease Disorders With**<br>**Known Causes**|<br>Polymyositis/dermatomyositis<br>Systemic lupus erythematosus|\n|Infectious/postinfectious processes<br>Adenovirus viruses<br>Influenza viruses<br>_Chlamydia pneumoniae_<br>Mycoplasma pneumoniae<br>Environmental agents<br>Hypersensitivity pneumonitis<br>Toxic inhalation<br>Aspiration syndromes|Rheumatoid arthritis including JIA<br>Lymphangioleiomyomatosis<br>Pulmonary hemangiomatosis<br>Neurocutaneous syndromes<br>Hermansky-Pudlak syndrome|\n|**Pulmonary Diseases Associated With Primary and**<br>**Secondary Immune Deficiency**<br>Opportunistic infections<br>Granulomatous lymphocytic interstitial lung disease associated<br>with common variable immunodeficiency syndrome<br>Lymphoid intestinal pneumonia (HIV infection)<br>Therapeutic interventions: chemotherapy, radiation,<br>transplantation, and rejection||\n|Modified from Deutsch GH, Young LR, Deterding RR, et al. Diffuse lung disease        i<br>_Care Med_. 2007;176:1120-1128. (Table 448.16 in_Nelson Textbook of Pediatric_<br>_GBM,_Glomerular basement membrane;_JIA,_juvenile idiopathic arthritis;_MAS,_m|i             in young children: application of a novel classification scheme._Am J Respir Crit_<br>_s_22/e.)<br>acrophage activation syndrome.|\n\n\nof affected individuals. Gastroesophageal reflux and pulmonary hypertension may be associated comorbidities. Chest wall deformities such as pectus excavatum are often present in individuals with **pleuro-parenchymal pulmonary fibroelastosis (PPFE)** , a rare form of pulmonary fibrosis that is mostly restricted to the upper lobes and disorders related to pathogenic variants in _ABCA3_ . Surfactant protein C and telomere-related pathogenic gene variants might present with failure to improve following viral respiratory tract infections, including respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) bronchiolitis. \n\n**Children older than 2 years:** progressive breathlessness during exercise, with or without cough, is a common presentation of chILD in older children and may initially be confused with asthma. Affected individuals typically present with hypoxemia, abnormal chest auscultation (e.g., inspiratory crackles or wheezes), and digital clubbing. Gastroesophageal reflux, aspiration, or pulmonary hypertension may be associated comorbidities. A positive family suggests monogenic ILDs while environmental exposures suggest hypersensitivity pneumonitis; these distinctions are not absolute because family members may share both\n\n<!-- p.562 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0563_01.jpg)\n\n\n##### **TABLE The Pediatric Interstitial Lung Diseases in 24.2 Children Over 2 Years of Age** \n\n**Disorders of the Immunocompetent Host** Disorders of Infancy \n\nGrowth abnormalities NEHI Disorders of surfactant metabolism \n\nSystemic Disease Immune-mediated disorders \n\n• Connective tissue disease related lung disease • Pulmonary hemorrhage syndromes Storage diseases Sarcoidosis \n\n**Disorders of the Immunocompromised Host** Opportunistic infections Related to treatment • Chemotherapy • Radiation Drug hypersensitivity Related to transplantation • Rejection • GVHD • PTLD Lymphoid infiltrates \n\n_GVHD,_ Graft-versus-host disease; _NEHI,_ neuroendocrine cell hyperplasia of infancy; _PTLD,_ posttransplant lymphoproliferative disease. Modified from Fan LL, Dishop MK, Galambos C, et al. Diffuse lung disease in biopsied children 2 to 18 years of age. Application of the chILD classification scheme. _Ann Am Thorac Soc_ . 2015;12(10):1498-1505. (Table 448.17 in _Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics_ 22/e.) \n\nenvironmental exposures and genetic risks. Specific etiologies can be suggested by certain extrapulmonary signs (e.g., skin, eye, or joint abnormalities) and initial laboratory investigations. \n\n#### **Diagnosis** \n\nOnce more common pediatric respiratory diseases such as cystic fibrosis, primary ciliary dyskinesia (see Chapter 25), immunodeficiency, infection, and recurrent aspiration have been excluded, evaluation for chILD should be considered in individuals with respiratory signs and symptoms, hypoxemia, and interstitial lung changes on chest imaging. Figure 24.1 (plus Tables 24.4, 24.5, and 24.6) summarizes the diagnostic algorithm. Plain chest radiography is commonly used but the information provided is limited and nonspecific and may be normal, especially early in disease progression. _Highresolution computed tomography (HRCT) of the chest should be the first-line imaging investigation because it allows for distinguishing broadly between chILD and noninterstitial lung diseases_ . The higher resolution permits visualization up to the level of the secondary pulmonary lobule, allowing for the identification of interstitial patterns and the delineation of their distribution, which can narrow the differential diagnosis (Table 24.4). Common interstitial patterns on HRCT include ground-glass attenuation (in some cases with \n\n##### **TABLE Clinical Features, Age, and Onset of Surfactant 24.3 Protein Dysfunction Syndromes (SPDS)** \n\n|SPDS|Clinical Features|Age and Onset|\n|---|---|---|\n|_SFTPB_|_Neonatal_<br>• Respiratory distress|Neonate, acute|\n|_ABCA3_|_Neonatal_<br>|Neonate, acute<br>|\n||• Respiratory distress<br>_Infancy_<br>|Infancy and<br>childhood,<br>|\n||• Cough|subacute|\n||• Tachypnoea,<br>|Late childhood<br>|\n||hypoxemia<br>• Failure to thrive<br>_Childhood_<br>|and adulthood,<br>chronic|\n||• Wheeze, crackles||\n||• Exercise intolerance||\n||• Dyspnea||\n||• Retractions,<br>crackles, digital<br>clubbing||\n||• Low body weight||\n|_SFTPC_|_Neonatal_|Neonate, acute|\n||• Respiratory<br>distress<br>|(infrequent)<br>Infancy and<br>|\n||_Childhood_|childhood,|\n||• Cough|subacute|\n||• Tachypnea,<br>|Late childhood<br>|\n||hypoxemia|and adulthood,<br>chronic|\n|_NKX2.1_|_Respiratory_|Any age|\n||• Neonatal<br>respiratory distress<br>• Recurrent infections<br>• Chronic interstitial<br>lung disease<br>_Neurological_<br>|Acute or chronic|\n||• Chorea<br>• Ataxia<br>• Developmental delay<br>• Hypotonia<br>||\n||• Hypothyroidism||\n|GMCSFR|_Infancy_|_Infancy_|\n||Respiratory distress<br>Cough|Chronic|\n|Anti-GMCSF|Respiratory|_Teenage years_|\n|antibodies|Cough<br>Exercise intolerance<br>Hypoxemia|Chronic|\n\n\n_ABCA3,_ ATP binding cassette number A3. \n\nFrom Gupta A, Zheng SL. Genetic disorders of surfactant protein dysfunction: when to consider and how to investigate. _Arch Dis Child_ . 2017;102:84-90, Table 2, p. 86. (Table 448-19 in _Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics_ 22/e.) \n\nintralobular lines), interlobular septal thickening, air cysts, and less frequently fibrotic signs such as honeycombing and traction bronchiectasis (Fig. 24.2). While HRCT typically cannot provide a specific diagnosis, the combination of HRCT findings, along with clinical history, laboratory evaluation, and, if needed, bronchoalveolar lavage and\n\n<!-- p.563 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0564_02.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 24.1** Investigations in childhood interstitial lung disease (chILD) diagnostic workup. (A) The investigations are performed one after the other to reach a chILD etiology. The first step is usually done by the general pediatrician _(framed in red)_ , and includes family history, clinical evaluation, chest radiography, and routine laboratory tests. (B) Age at onset is crucial information that helps to consider some etiologies more than others. (C) Bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) eliminates infectious causes of ILD and provides clues to the most likely diagnoses. (D) When thoracic computed tomography (CT) scan, specific blood tests, BAL, and genetic analyses could not assess a diagnosis, lung biopsy is the last step of the diagnostic process, allowing to assess the most likely diagnoses in most cases. _AEC2,_ Type 2 alveolar epithelial cells; _DAH,_ diffuse alveolar hemorrhage; _HSP,_ hypersensitivity pneumonitis; _LIP,_ lymphoid interstitial pneumonia; _NEHI,_ neuroendocrine cell hyperplasia of infancy; _NSIP,_ nonspecific interstitial pneumonia; _OB,_ obliterans bronchiolitis; _PAP,_ pulmonary alveolar proteinosis; _PAS,_ Periodic acid–Schiff.  (From Nathan N, Griese M, Michel K, et al. Diagnostic workup of childhood interstitial lung disease. _Eur Respir Rev_ . 2023;32:220188 [Fig. 1].)\n\n<!-- p.564 -->\n\n##### **TABLE Childhood Interstitial Lung Disease Diagnostic Assessment Based on Computed Tomography 24.4 Scan Pattern** \n\n|Elementary Lesions|Distribution|Suspected Diagnoses|\n|---|---|---|\n|GGO|Dense, diffuse|Inherited surfactant disorders|\n|GGO, peripheral traction cysts|Diffuse|Inherited surfactant disorders|\n|GGO, peripheral and/or parenchymal<br>traction cysts, traction<br>bronchiectasis, reticulations||Inherited surfactant disorders (older age);<br>autoinflammatory disorders|\n|Diffuse (sometimes ill-defined<br>centrilobular) nodules, diffuse<br>GGO±alveolar consolidation|Patchy|Diffuse alveolar hemorrhage|\n|GGO, cysts, honeycombing and<br>reticulations|Peripheral|Connective tissue diseases, systemic and<br>autoimmune diseases|\n|GGO|Paramediastinal, paracardial, middle<br>lobe, lingula (usual); others (aberrant)|Persistent tachypnoea of infancy/<br>neuroendocrine cell hyperplasia of infancy|\n|GGO and air trapping|Centrilobular|Hypersensitivity pneumonitis|\n|Reversed halo sign||Organizing pneumonia|\n|Crazy paving|More intense in lower lobes|Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis|\n|Micronodules, hilar lymphadenopathies|Lymphatic distribution|Sarcoidosis|\n\n\n_GGO,_ Ground-glass opacities. Nathan N, Griese M, Michel K, et al. Diagnostic workup of childhood interstitial lung disease. _Eur Respir Rev_ . 2023; 32: 220188 [DOI:10.1183/16000617.0188-2022]. \n\nbiopsy studies, can often establish a precise diagnosis. In some instances, the imaging findings on HRCT _are_ consistent with specific causes of chILD, such as **neuroendocrine cell hyperplasia of infancy (NEHI)** , with ground-glass opacities restricted almost entirely to the middle lobes (Figs. 24.2A and 24.3), **pulmonary alveolar proteinosis** (ground-glass opacities with smooth thickening of interlobular and intralobular septal lines, termed the _crazy-paving pattern_ and depicted in Figures 24.2E and 24.4), **pulmonary alveolar microlithiasis** (diffuse ground-glass attenuation and multiple subpleural linear calcifications as depicted in Fig. 24.5) and **acute hypersensitivity pneumonitis** (upper lung predominant pulmonary changes including groundglass opacities, poorly defined centrilobular nodules, and areas of decreased attenuation representing air trapping, depicted in Figs. 24.2F and 24.6). \n\nFollowing HRCT, the next phases of the diagnostic evaluation should optimize the amount of information gained from noninvasive testing, including blood tests (e.g., inflammatory markers, blood counts, serum chemistries, autoimmune antibodies, and genetic testing) and pulmonary function testing (Table 24.5). The role of pulmonary function testing in diagnosing chILD is not well defined. Certain conditions may be more likely to demonstrate a restrictive lung disease pattern, while others are more associated with an obstructive pattern. When such testing has demonstrated abnormalities, serial pulmonary function tests may be helpful in monitoring disease progression and response to therapy. Genetic etiologies are increasingly identified in chILDs, including surfactant protein \n\ndefects, telomere-related disorders, COPA syndrome, and others; up to 20% of chILDs either have or are believed to have a genetic cause (Table 24.6). When chest imaging and noninvasive tests are inconclusive, invasive procedures are needed and include laryngotracheal bronchoscopy (LTBS) and surgical lung biopsy. LTBS enables anatomic evaluation and the collection of samples for histopathological and microbiological examination, including bronchial aspiration samples, bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL), bronchial brushing, bronchial biopsy, and transbronchial biopsy (through conventional forceps or cryoprobe). The BAL study results may suggest specific diseases, like diffuse alveolar hemorrhage or alveolar proteinosis (Fig. 24.1). \n\nTransbronchial or video-assisted thoracoscopic lung biopsy is reserved for instances where diagnosis remains elusive and histopathological investigation is required (Figs. 24.7 and 24.8), though indications are narrowing given the increased yield of genetic testing. In young children, a thoracoscopic or open lung biopsy is recommended; in adolescents, cryobiopsy is increasingly employed. \n\n#### **Classification of Childhood Interstitial Lung Diseases** \n\nThe prognosis for children with ILD varies widely depending on the specific diagnosis, the extent of lung involvement, and the effectiveness of treatment. Some affected individuals may stabilize, improve, or even completely \n\n_(Text continued on page 549)_\n\n<!-- p.565 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0566_01.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 24.2** Selected illustrative thoracic high-resolution computed tomography images in childhood interstitial lung disease. (A) Neuroendocrine cell hyperplasia of infancy: ground-glass opacification typically involving the right middle lobe and lingula; (B) Pulmonary interstitial glycogenosis: nonspecific and nonuniform ground-glass opacification and mosaic attenuation; (C) Surfactant protein C deficiency: diffuse ground-glass opacities with occasional cystic changes; (D) _ABCA3_ gene variant: ground-glass opacification with septal thickening and cystic changes; (E) Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis: scattered opacification with intralobular and interlobular septal thickening (the “crazy paving” pattern); (F) Hypersensitivity pneumonitis: ground-glass opacification with mosaic attenuation; (G) _FARSB_ pathogenic variant: diffuse opacification with subpleural and parenchymal cystic changes; (H) _TMEM173_ pathogenic variant _STING_ -associated vasculopathy with onset in infancy (SAVI): diffuse opacification with septal thickening and cystic changes.\n\n<!-- p.566 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0567_02.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 24.3** Neuroendocrine cell hyperplasia of infancy, high-resolution chest CT findings. (A) Axial image shows patchy ground-glass opacification involving the right middle lobe _(long black arrow)_ and lingula _(short black arrow)_ . (B) Ground-glass opacities are likewise predominantly central in location _(arrows)_ . (C) Coronal image shows areas of air-trapping _(white arrows)_ .  (From Lee J, Sanchez TR, Zhang Y, Jhawar S. The role of high-resolution chest CT in the diagnosis of neuroendocrine cell hyperplasia of infancy: a rare form of pediatric interstitial lung disease. _Respir Med Case Rep._ 2015;16:101-103, Elsevier.) \n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0567_04.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 24.4** Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis. Axial CT chest scan demonstrating the “crazy paving” pattern, consisting of ground-glass opacification with superimposed intralobular and interlobular septal thickening. (From Meka SG, Mohr M, Nair GB, Salman BA. Autoimmune pulmonary alveolar proteinosis mimicking _Mycolplasma_ pneumonia in an adolescent. _Respir Med Case Rep._ 2020;30:101100, Elsevier.)\n\n<!-- p.567 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0568_01.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 24.5** Pulmonary alveolar microlithiasis. High-resolution computed tomographic images of a 31-yearold female patient with pulmonary alveolar microlithiasis. Images obtained at the levels of the superior (A) and inferior (B) lobes with an intermediary window show hyperdense consolidations in both lungs caused by the confluence of calcified nodules.  (From Ferreira Francisco FA, Pereira e Silva JL, Hochhegger B, Zanetti G, Marchiori E. Pulmonary alveolar microlithiasis. State-of-the-art review. _Respir Med._ 2013;107:1-9, Elsevier.) \n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0568_03.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 24.6** Hypersensitivity pneumonitis. Two examples of three-density sign in patients with nonfibrotic hypersensitivity pneumonitis. (A) Inspiratory CT imaging shows patchy ground-glass attenuation in the anterior left upper lobe _(blue circle)_ . Other lobules are of preserved (normal) attenuation _(yellow circle)_ , and there is lobular decreased attenuation in the posterior left lung _(red circle)_ . No signs of fibrosis are present. (B) Expiratory CT imaging accentuates numerous lobules of decreased attenuation representing air-trapping. (C) Inspiratory CT imaging in a different patient shows diffuse ground-glass attenuation _(blue circle)_ . An adjacent lobule is of preserved (normal) attenuation _(yellow circle)_ , and there are multiple lobules with decreased attenuation _(red circle)_ . No signs of fibrosis are present. (D) Expiratory CT imaging accentuates numerous lobules of decreased attenuation representing air-trapping.  (From Fernandez Perez ER, Travis WD, et al. Diagnosis and evaluation of hypersensitivity pneumonitis: CHEST guideline and expert panel report. _Chest_ . 2021;160:e97-e156, Elsevier.)\n\n<!-- p.568 -->\n\n###### **TABLE** \n\n##### **Investigations in Childhood Interstitial Lung Disease** \n\n|**24.5**<br>**g    g**||\n|---|---|\n|Investigation|Indicates|\n|**Hematology**<br>Complete blood count||\n|Reticulocytes|Anemia and/or diffuse alveolar hemorrhage|\n|Hemostasis|Anemia and/or diffuse alveolar hemorrhage|\n|**Biochemistry**<br>Serum electrolytes, creatinine||\n|Liver enzymes|Hepatomegaly and/or pulmonary alveolar proteinosis|\n|Thyroxine, thyroid-stimulating hormone|Surfactant disorder_(NKX2-1)_|\n|Serum protein electrophoresis, sedimentation rate|Autoinflammatory/inflammatory disorder|\n|Angiotensin-converting enzyme|Sarcoidosis|\n|Iron, ferritin|Anemia and/or diffuse alveolar hemorrhage and inflammatory<br>syndrome, HLH if elevated ferritin|\n|Calcium, ionized calcium, phosphorous|Sarcoidosis|\n|Lactate dehydrogenase|Alveolar lung injury|\n|Proteinuria|Autoinflammatory/inflammatory disorder|\n|Hypercalciuria|Sarcoidosis|\n|Hyperammonemia||\n|Blood and urine amino acids|Metabolic disorder, (e.g., lysinuric proteinuria)|\n|Urinary organic acids||\n|**Serologies**||\n|Epstein–Barr virus serology and viral load||\n|Cytomegalovirus serology and viral load|If subacute, neonatal or immune deficiency|\n|HIV-1/HIV-2 serology and viral load|Pneumocystis jirovecii, immune deficiency|\n|_Mycoplasma pneumoniae_serology and nasopharyngeal PCR|Subacute|\n|_Chlamydia pneumoniae_serology and nasopharyngeal PCR|Subacute|\n|_Chlamydia trachomatis_serology and nasopharyngeal PCR|Subacute in newborns|\n|_Ureaplasma urealyticum_serology and nasopharyngeal PCR||\n|IgG precipitins|Hypersensitivity pneumonitis, farmer’s lung, bird fancier’s lung|\n|**Immunology**|i|\n|Postvaccinal serologies|Immune deficiency|\n|IgG, IgA, IgM, and IgG subclasses|Immune deficiency, autoinflammatory/inflammatory disorder|\n|C3, C4, CH50||\n|Lymphocyte count/differential||\n|Circulating immune complexes||\n|Antinuclear antibodies||\n|ANCAc (PR3), ANCAp (MPO)||\n|Rheumatoid factor||\n|Anti-CCP||\n|Anticardiolipin||\n\n\n_(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.569 -->\n\n##### **TABLE 24.5 Investigations in Childhood Interstitial Lung Disease—cont’d** \n\n|Investigation|Indicates|\n|---|---|\n|Scleroderma, polymyositis, and myositis antibodies<br>(KU, PM-Scl75, TIF1γ, MDA-5, PM-Scl100, Ml2, KJ;<br>antisynthetase (PL7, PL12, OJ, centromere, SRP, JO1),<br>smooth muscle, glomerular basement membrane)|Muscular, esophageal, and/or cutaneous involvement|\n|GM-CSF autoantibodies|Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis|\n|IFN-αsignature, IFN-αdosage|Immune deficiency, autoinflammatory/inflammatory disorder|\n|Genetic|WES/WGS; specific panels|\n\n\n_ANCA,_ Antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody; _CCP,_ cyclic citrullinated peptide; _GM-CSF,_ granulocyte–macrophage colony-stimulating factor; _HLH,_ hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis; _IFN,_ interferon; _MPO,_ myeloperoxidase; _PR3,_ proteinase 3; _WES,_ whole exome sequencing; _WGS,_ whole genome sequencing. Nathan N, Griese M, Michel K, et al. Diagnostic workup of childhood interstitial lung disease. _Eur Respir Rev._ 2023; 32: 220188 [DOI:10.1183/16000617.0188-2022]. \n\n##### **TABLE Main Genes and Proteins Currently Implicated in Childhood Interstitial Lung Disease (chILD) Groups, 24.6 Their Mode of Transmission, and the Associated Phenotypes** \n\n|Gene|Inheritance Pattern|Phenotypes|\n|---|---|---|\n|**Inherited Surfactan**|**t Disorders**||\n|_SFTPA1, SFTPA2_|AD|Very rarely chILD, adult ILD, and adenocarcinoma of the lung|\n|_SFTPB_|AR|Neonatal respiratory distress±PH|\n|_SFTPC_|AD|Neonatal respiratory distress; ILD in infants or children, adults|\n|_ABCA3_|AR|Neonatal respiratory distress±PH; ILD in infants or children, adults|\n|_NKX2-1_|AD|Brain–lung–thyroid syndrome|\n|**PAP**|||\n|_MARS_|AR|PAP; hepatomegaly with cholestasis, anemia, neurological impairment|\n|_CSF2RA, CSF2RB_|GR and AR|PAP (infants, children, adults)|\n|_GATA2_|AR|Secondary PAP; immune deficiency with myelodysplasia|\n|**Autoinflammatory **|**Disorders**|l|\n|_TMEM173_|AD|Early chILD with autoimmune and inflammatory disease±joint and skin<br>involvement|\n|_COPA_|AD|Early chILD or DAH with autoimmune and inflammatory disease±joint and<br>kidney involvement|\n|_ZNFX1_||chILD with severe viral infections, neurological symptoms, thrombotic<br>microangiopathy|\n|_OAS1_|AD|PAP with immunodeficiency and autoinflammation|\n|**Other chILD**|||\n|_FLNA_|GA and GD|chILD with emphysema; cardiac abnormalities, neurologic impairment;<br>girls>boys|\n|_NHLRC2_|AR|FINCA|\n|**Diffuse Abnormaliti**|**es of Lung Development**||\n|_FOXF1_|AD|chILD with PH; alveolar capillary dysplasia±misalignment of pulmonary<br>veins|\n|_TBX4, FGFR2_|AD and AR|chILD with PH; acinar dysplasia|\n|_EIF2AK4_|AR|chILD with PH; pulmonary hemangiomatosis; veno-occlusive disease|\n\n\n_AD,_ Autosomal dominant; _AR,_ autosomal recessive; _DAH,_ diffuse alveolar hemorrhage; _FINCA,_ fibrosis, neurodegeneration, and cerebral angiomatosis; _GD,_ gonosomal dominant; _GR,_ gonosomal recessive; _PAP,_ pulmonary alveolar proteinosis; _PH,_ pulmonary hypertension. Nathan N, Griese M, Michel K, et al. Diagnostic workup of childhood interstitial lung disease. _Eur Respir Rev._ 2023; 32: 220188 [DOI:10.1183/16000617.0188-2022].\n\n<!-- p.570 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0571_02.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 24.7** Selected histological images of lung biopsies in childhood interstitial lung disease (I). (A) Pulmonary interstitial glycogenosis: alveolar simplification and septal thickening with increased mesenchymal cells, hematoxylin-eosin; (B) Pulmonary interstitial glycogenosis: detail of septal thickening with increased mesenchymal cells, hematoxylin-eosin; (C) Congenital alveolar dysplasia with glycogenosis: marked widening of the interstitium and obstruction of the preacinar and intraacinar arteries consistent with severe alveolar developmental disorder; (D) Congenital alveolar dysplasia with glycogenosis: detail of interstitial widening with mesenchymal cells staining positive for glycogen; (E) Chronic pneumonitis of infancy: significant interstitial thickening due to proliferation of fibroblastic and lymphomonocytic cells; (F) Chronic pneumonitis of infancy: detail of type II pneumocyte hyperplasia. \n\nrecover with treatment (e.g., NEHI and acute hypersensitivity pneumonitis), while others may progressively worsen and even die despite numerous interventions (e.g., **neonatal surfactant protein B deficiency** and **dyskeratosis congenita** ). Poor prognostic factors include chILD due to diffuse developmental disorders and surfactant protein defects, onset of disease younger than 6 months of age, and baseline oxygen saturation less than 94%. The frequency and extent of follow-up evaluations depend on the type and severity of disease; collaboration and proper transition between pediatric pulmonologists and adult ILD specialists \n\nare crucial for the optimal management of those patients that survive beyond childhood. \n\n## **Diffuse Developmental Disorders** \n\nPulmonary diffuse developmental disorders are severe alterations of lung structure due to failure in one of the primary molecular mechanisms of lung parenchymal and vascular development. Disorders include **acinar dysplasia (AcDys)** , **congenital alveolar dysplasia** ( **CAD** ; Fig. 24.7C–D), and **alveolar-capillary dysplasia with misalignment of the**\n\n<!-- p.571 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0572_01.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 24.8** Selected additional histological images of lung biopsies in childhood interstitial lung disease (II). (A) Niemann-Pick disease: total occupation of alveolar spaces, pneumocyte hyperplasia, and septal thickening; (B) Niemann-Pick disease: detail of alveolar proteinosis pattern with lipoproteinaceous material in intra-alveolar macrophages; (C) Neuroendocrine cell hyperplasia of infancy: image of isolated neuroendocrine cells in the bronchiolar wall, Bombesin staining; (D) STING-associated vasculopathy with onset in infancy (SAVI): pattern of lymphocytic interstitial pneumonia and follicular bronchiolitis with septal thickening, T and B lymphocytic aggregates in the interstitium and airways; (E and F) Hermansky-Pudlak syndrome: interstitial pneumonia pattern; predominant subpleural fibrotic pattern with microscopic honeycombing, combined with areas of normal lung (E), and the presence of multiple fibroblastic foci in the fibrotic regions (F). \n\n**pulmonary veins (ACDMPV)** . Newborns present with severe refractory hypoxemic respiratory failure, usually associated with pulmonary hypertension. The prognosis of these conditions is poor, and lung transplantation is rarely feasible, though milder forms of ACDMPV with delayed presentation have been reported. \n\n_De novo_ or inherited autosomal dominant pathogenic variants in _FOXF1_ are responsible for ACDMPV; rare cases have been described associated with homozygous autosomal recessive pathogenic variants in _STRA6_ . The genetic basis of \n\nAcDys and CAD is more complex and includes pathogenic variants in _TBX4_ , _FGF10_ , and _FGFR2_ . \n\n## **Pulmonary Interstitial Glycogenosis** \n\nPulmonary interstitial glycogenosis (PIG) is a disease of infants, typically presenting before the age of 1 year and often in the neonatal period with tachypnea and hypoxemia requiring supplemental oxygen or, in severe cases, mechanical ventilation. HRCT shows a patchy ground-glass pattern,\n\n<!-- p.572 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0573_01.jpg)\n\n\nseptal thickening, and air trapping; thick parenchymal bands and architectural distortion of the lung parenchyma may also be seen (Fig. 24.2B). Histologically, periodic acidSchiff (PAS) staining or electron microscopy demonstrates interstitial thickening secondary to infiltration by poorly differentiated mesenchymal cells with cytoplasmic deposits of glycogen; variable grades of alveolar simplification may also be seen (Fig. 24.7A–B). PIG is considered a _lung dysmaturity disorder_ with altered lung development and dysfunction in the production of fetal lung surfactant. \n\nTwo groups of affected individuals can be distinguished: (1) glycogenosis restricted solely to the lung without known syndromic or genetic associations, and (2) symptomatic pulmonary glycogenosis as part of a syndromic genetic disorder, often with congenital pulmonary pathologies and/or cardiac malformations. In isolated PIG, symptoms usually disappear with time, though long-term radiological and pulmonary function sequelae may persist. Individuals with other associated comorbidities, or with significant pulmonary developmental disorders, often have a poorer prognosis related to the associated anomalies. There is no established treatment; short courses of systemic corticosteroids are recommended. \n\n## **Neuroendocrine Cell Hyperplasia of Infancy** \n\nInitially called _persistent tachypnea of infancy_ and later renamed neuroendocrine cell hyperplasia of infancy (NEHI) due to the increased number of neuroendocrine cells found in the airways in biopsies (Fig. 24.8C), the pathophysiology of this disease is unknown. Clinically, NEHI is characterized by tachypnea, retractions, crackles, and hypoxemia, which typically appear between 2 and 6 months of age. Clubbing is absent, as are cough and wheezing at baseline. Extrapulmonary manifestations of NEHI include failure to thrive and developmental delay. HRCT imaging typically shows air trapping and ground-glass opacities, predominantly in the middle lobe, lingula, and perihilar regions (Figs. 24.2A and 24.3). \n\nHistorically, diagnosis was based on lung biopsy demonstrating large areas of normal lung with an increased number of neuroendocrine cells. Currently, the use of biopsy is much more limited and a diagnosis of NEHI is usually established through characteristic HRCT images and clinical findings. Scoring systems to facilitate clinical diagnosis have been proposed, in which 10 items are evaluated: symptom onset before 12 months of age, failure to thrive, absence of clubbing, absence of baseline cough, absence of baseline wheeze, presence of chest wall abnormalities (e.g., barrel chest or pectus excavatum), crackles, hypoxemia, tachypnea, and retractions. The presence of 7 out of 10 items suggests NEHI, although the specificity of this scoring system has not been established. \n\nInitial treatment is mainly supportive and consists of oxygen therapy and nutritional support. Systemic or inhaled corticosteroids are not recommended. Clinical course is generally favorable, though it can vary; affected individuals tend to \n\nimprove and even recover within a few years due to the regression of the neuroendocrine cells over time. \n\n## **Surfactant Protein Deficiencies** \n\nPulmonary surfactant, produced by type II pneumocytes in the alveoli, is a mixture of lipids and proteins that reduces alveolar surface tension, preventing the alveoli from collapsing during expiration. Proteins make up approximately 10% of surfactant and are divided into four types: surfactant protein A (SP-A), surfactant protein B (SP-B), surfactant protein C (SP-C), and surfactant protein D (SP-D). Other crucial proteins include ABCA3 and TTF1. ABCA3 has an essential role in surfactant lipid transport, while TTF1 is essential in surfactant protein transcription. Inheritance, histological findings, clinical signs and symptoms, and prognosis of each surfactant protein deficiency vary depending on the protein affected (Table 24.3) (see Chapter 26). \n\n## **_Surfactant Protein B Deficiency_** \n\nSP-B is a hydrophobic protein encoded by _SFTPB_ , located on chromosome 2. Surfactant protein B deficiency is autosomal recessively inherited; clinical manifestations appear at birth and consist of rapidly progressive respiratory failure that leads to death in the first few months of life, even with optimal medical treatment and the administration of exogenous surfactant. Longer survival is only observed in milder phenotypes with partial residual protein production. \n\n## **_Surfactant Protein C Deficiency_** \n\nSP-C is a hydrophobic protein encoded by _SFTPC_ , located on chromosome 2. **Surfactant protein C deficiency** is autosomal dominantly inherited or caused by a _de novo_ pathogenic variant. Manifestations vary widely, typically beginning in childhood, though some affected individuals present with pulmonary fibrosis in the fifth or sixth decade of life. Primary findings include tachypnea and hypoxemia, which may be accompanied by failure to thrive. HRCT typically demonstrates diffuse ground-glass opacities with occasional cystic changes (Fig. 24.2C). Prognosis is variable, with some individuals improving after needing supplementary oxygen for a period of time. In other cases, individuals may develop respiratory failure requiring lung transplantation. \n\n## **_Surfactant Protein A Deficiency_** \n\nSP-A is encoded by 2 genes on chromosome 10 ( _SFTPA1_ and _SFTPA2_ ). Pathogenic variants in these genes causing **surfactant protein A deficiency** have been associated with pulmonary fibrosis and pulmonary adenocarcinoma in adults. \n\n## **_ABCA3 Deficiency_** \n\nABCA3 is encoded by _ABCA3_ , located on chromosome 16. **ABCA3 deficiency** is autosomal recessively inherited and is the most common cause of surfactant protein deficiency.\n\n<!-- p.573 -->\n\nComplete loss of protein function presents as neonatal respiratory distress with a clinical course similar to that of surfactant protein B deficiency. Partial deficiency presents after the neonatal period and follows a milder course; some individuals stabilize and even improve. Other individuals present in late childhood or adulthood with cough, dyspnea, reduced exercise tolerance, and chest wall deformities. HRCT findings include ground-glass opacification with septal thickening and cystic changes (Fig. 24.2D). \n\n## **_NKX2-1 Deficiency_** \n\n_NKX2-1_ is located on chromosome 14 and encodes thyroid transcription factor 1, which plays a critical role in thyroid and lung development, and which is also expressed in the basal ganglia. Pathogenic variants in _NKX2-1_ are associated with pulmonary manifestations, neurological abnormalities (mostly chorea), and hypothyroidism, a phenotypic constellation known as **brain-lung-thyroid syndrome** . In approximately 40% of cases all three organs are affected, in 30% two organs are affected, while 6% of cases present with pulmonary manifestations alone. Pulmonary manifestations are variable, although neonatal respiratory distress is common. There are some forms with more chronic involvement, in which recurrent pulmonary infections are a characteristic feature. \n\n## **_Histopathology of Surfactant Protein Deficiencies_** \n\nHistological patterns are variable, although the most characteristic findings are type II pneumocyte hyperplasia, interstitial thickening, foamy macrophages, and the presence of proteinaceous material in the distal air spaces. Each protein deficiency type has other, more specific, characteristics. In SP-B deficiency, congenital alveolar proteinosis or desquamative interstitial pneumonitis may be found. In SP-C deficiency, chronic pneumonitis of infancy may be seen, especially in individuals under 2 years of age. The main findings in ABCA3 deficiency are alveolar proteinosis, desquamative interstitial pneumonitis, and nonspecific pneumonitis. Under electron microscopy, abnormalities in lamellar bodies can be observed in SP-B and ABCA3 deficiency; lamellar bodies are smaller and denser in ABCA3 deficiency. \n\n## **_Treatment of Surfactant Protein Deficiencies_** \n\nGiven the rarity of these disorders, evidence-based treatment guidelines are lacking. The first line of treatment is generally high-dose systemic corticosteroids, administered intravenously or by mouth. Other medical treatments include hydroxychloroquine and azithromycin, though for many affected individuals, lung transplantation is the only option. The age at transplantation depends on the severity of each case. Active areas of investigation include targeted gene therapy and stem cell therapy. \n\n## **Telomere Disorders** \n\nHereditary telomere disorders are due to pathogenic variants in genes that encode telomerase complex proteins and are characterized by abnormalities in maintaining telomere \n\nlength, which is essential for proper cell function and proliferation. These disorders vary in symptomatology and age of presentation but may have overlapping phenotypes with incomplete penetrance and variable expressivity. Telomere dysfunction and shortening are present in germline cells; therefore, multiple organs and systems are often involved even if symptoms related to a specific tissue predominate. Childhood-onset telomere disorders were the first recognized, though adult-onset diseases including forms of pulmonary fibrosis have subsequently been identified. Phenotypic clusters and gene variants are included in Table 24.7. Telomere disorders in childhood may present with the complete form of dyskeratosis congenita or with singleorgan-predominant involvement, such as immunodeficiency or bone marrow failure in the first decade of life or pulmonary fibrosis in the second decade of life. \n\n## **_Dyskeratosis Congenita_** \n\nDyskeratosis congenita (DC) is an inherited bone marrow failure and cancer predisposition syndrome caused by defects in telomere maintenance and characterized by the clinical triad of abnormal nails, reticular skin pigmentation, and oral leukoplakia. Affected individuals may also develop aplastic anemia, myelodysplastic syndrome, leukemia, solid tumors, and pulmonary fibrosis, especially in the second decade of life. Diagnosis requires recognizing typical clinical features and evaluating telomere length from blood leukocytes via flow fluorescence in situ hybridization, quantitative PCR, or Southern blot, as well as identifying pathogenic gene variants. Telomere shortening is present in all patients, although telomerase-related pathogenic gene variants are not always identified. Proper diagnosis is critical, as bone marrow failure in individuals with DC does not respond to immunosuppression, and hematopoietic stem cell transplantation is associated with increased rates of morbidity and mortality relative to individuals undergoing transplantation for non-DC-related bone marrow failure. Failure to respond to immunosuppressive therapy in bone marrow failure may be the first indicator of DC if other clinical signs or symptoms are lacking. \n\nManagement is complex and based on patient-specific needs and disease features. Some disease features have responded to androgen therapy such as danazol, though data are inconclusive and doses often require close monitoring and adjustment to minimize side effects such as impaired liver function, virilization, and behavioral problems. Despite morbidity and mortality concerns, hematopoietic stem-cell transplantation is often required in individuals with severe bone marrow failure and other hematological disorders. Fibrotic ILD often presents in the second decade of life; data on the efficacy of anti-fibrotic medications in DC are lacking. Additional manifestations can include gastrointestinal problems, esophageal stenosis requiring dilatation, liver fibrosis, osteopenia, avascular necrosis of the hips, and ophthalmic problems including lacrimal obstruction, corneal erosions, and retinopathy. \n\nThere are two ultra-rare and severe forms of DC: (1) **Hoyeraal-Hreidarsson syndrome** , characterized by cerebellar hypoplasia, microcephaly, developmental delay,\n\n<!-- p.574 -->\n\n\n#### **TABLE 24.7** Telomer e Disorders : Associated Ge nes, Inheritance Patterns, and Clinical Features\n\n|Disease|Genes|Inheritance|Possible Clinical Features|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Dyskeratosis<br>Congenita<br>(DC)|_DKC1_<br>_TERT_<br>_TERC_<br>_TINF2_<br>_NHP2_|XLR<br>AD, AR<br>AD<br>AD<br>AR, AD|Lacy, reticular skin pigmentation (subtle or diffuse), abnormal fingernails and<br>toenails, early gray hair or hair loss, hyperhidrosis<br>Immunodeficiency<br>Aplastic anemia, myelodysplastic syndrome, leukemia<br>Intrauterine growth retardation|\n||_NOP10_|AD, AR|Epiphora, blepharitis, sparse eyelashes, ectro/entropion, retinopathy<br>Oral leukoplakia, dental caries, periodontal disease, taurodontism<br>Squamous cell head and neck cancer<br>Cardiomyopathy<br>Fibrotic ILD (cough, dyspnea)<br>Esophageal stenosis, enteropathy, liver fibrosis<br>Microcephaly, intracranial calcifications, cerebellar hypoplasia, schizophrenia|\n|Fibrotic ILD<br>without DC<br>criteria|_DKC1_<br>_RTEL1_<br>_TINF2_<br>_PARN_<br>_NAF1_<br>_ZCCHC8_<br>_RPA1_<br>_POT1_<br>_NOP10_<br>_NHP2_<br>_ACD_|XLR<br>AD, AR<br>AD<br>AR<br>AD<br>AD<br>AD<br>AD<br>AD, AR<br>AD, AR<br>AD, AR|Cough, dyspnea<br>HRCT pattern: UIP, NSIP, HP, PPFE, Indeterminate<br>Frequent association with other clinical disorders (personal<br>or familial); anemia or hematological dysplasia, liver fibrosis, immunological<br>disorders, early gray hair, cancer at young age|\n\n\nInterstitial lung disease (ILD) is the most frequent clinical manifestation of telomere disorders. However, children with severe telomere shortening and telomeraserelated pathogenic gene variants may manifest dyskeratosis congenita with wide variations in clinical severity, with or without organ predominance (such as aplastic anemia). _AD,_ Autosomal dominant; _AR,_ autosomal recessive; _HP,_ hypersensitivity pneumonitis; _HRCT,_ high-resolution computed tomography; _NSIP,_ nonspecific interstitial pneumonia; _PPFE,_ pleuroparenchymal fibroelastosis; _UIP,_ unusual interstitial pneumonia (characterized by honeycombing, reticular opacities, ground-glass opacities, lung architectural distortion, and lobar volume loss); _XLR,_ X-linked recessive. \n\nimmunodeficiency, intrauterine growth restriction, and bone marrow failure, and (2) **Revesz syndrome** , affecting young children with bone marrow failure and exudative retinopathy, with additional features of sparse, fine hair, nail dystrophy, oral leukoplakia, central nervous system calcifications, and cerebellar hypoplasia. \n\n## **Multisystemic Diseases With Immunological Involvement** \n\nInterstitial lung involvement is common in many genetic immunodeficiencies. The most common presentations are follicular bronchiolitis, lymphoid interstitial pneumonitis, and granulomatous lymphocytic interstitial lung disease. Treatment typically consists of the antiinflammatory and/or immunomodulating medication recommended for the underlying systemic disease. Several forms of interstitial lung disease associated with autoinflammatory diseases or immune dysregulation have been described: \n\n## **_SAVI Syndrome (STING-Associated Vasculopathy With Onset in Infancy)_** \n\n_TMEM173_ encodes the cGAS-STING (cyclic GMP-AMP synthase stimulator of interferon genes) DNA sensor. \n\nDominant gain-of-function pathogenic variants in _TMEM173_ result in hyperproduction of interferon through the JAKkinase pathway. HRCT findings are presented in Figure 24.2H and representative histopathology is shown in Figure 24.8D. \n\nThe most frequent clinical manifestations are cutaneous vasculitis of the face, extremities, and acral areas (e.g., pinnae, nose, fingers) and diffuse pulmonary involvement in over 90% of cases. Arthritis and signs of systemic inflammation (e.g., chronic anemia, growth delays, fever) may be present, as well as the autoantibodies antinuclear (ANA), anticardiolipin, anti-β2 glycoprotein, antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies (ANCA). \n\n## **_COPA Syndrome_** \n\nCOPA syndrome is an autoinflammatory and autoimmune disease caused by autosomal dominantly inherited pathogenic variants in _COPA_ , the protein product of which—COPα—is responsible for transiting molecular cargo from the Golgi complex to the endoplasmic reticulum. Clinical manifestations include pulmonary hemorrhage or interstitial pneumonia (lymphocytic interstitial pneumonia or follicular bronchiolitis), arthritis, and renal disease (glomerulopathy in up to 44% of affected individuals). Autoantibodies including ANA, ANCA and\n\n<!-- p.575 -->\n\nrheumatoid factor (RF) are detected in up to 86% of affected individuals. There is also an increase in proinflammatory cytokines IL-1β, IL-6, IL-23, as well as activation of interferon type I. \n\n## **_Additional Syndromes_** \n\nAdditional disorders of immune dysregulation associated with chILD include **_STAT1_ gain-of-function disease** and **_STAT3_ gain-of-function disease** and CTLA-4 pathway defects ( **_CTLA-4_ haploinsufficiency** and **LRBA deficiency** ). Other genetic autoinflammatory disorders associated with chILD include **_ZNFX1_ deficiency** , which causes ILD with severe viral infections, neurological symptoms, and thrombotic microangiopathy, as well as **_OAS1_ deficiency** , which presents with alveolar proteinosis and immunodeficiency. \n\nSAVI syndrome, COPA syndrome, and STAT gain-offunction disease can be treated with JAK inhibitors; CTLA-4 pathway defects can be treated with the immunomodulator abatacept. \n\n# **Inherited Systemic Diseases and Syndromes Associated With Interstitial Lung Disease** \n\n## **Hermansky-Pudlak Syndrome** \n\nHermansky-Pudlak syndrome (HPS) is a rare multisystem hereditary disorder characterized by oculocutaneous albinism, a bleeding diathesis due to platelet aggregation defects, and other manifestations including neutropenia and granulomatous colitis (Fig. 24.8E–F). Pulmonary fibrosis in adolescents is a feature in HPS-1, which is caused by autosomal recessively inherited pathogenic variants in _HPS-1_ ; pulmonary fibrosis in children is a feature in HPS-2, which is \n\ncaused by autosomal recessively inherited pathogenic variants in _AP3B1_ . \n\n## **Filamin A Disease** \n\n_FLNA_ , located on the X chromosome, encodes filamin A, a cytoskeletal actin-binding protein crucial for cell signaling, migration, division, and motility. Pathogenic variants in _FLNA_ are associated with **X-linked periventricular nodular heterotopia** , a disorder characterized by neuronal migration abnormalities (particularly **cerebral periventricular nodular heterotopia** ), skeletal dysplasia, cardiovascular malformations, intellectual disability, and intestinal pseudo-obstruction (Fig. 24.9). Respiratory pathology includes interstitial lung disease and pulmonary hypertension. HRCT shows multilobe hyperinflation, emphysema, and pulmonary vascular attenuation (i.e., pulmonary arteriopathy). Respiratory disease severity ranges from asymptomatic to mild _childhood_ -onset or _adult_ -onset respiratory symptoms to severe _neonatal-infantile_ -onset respiratory failure that is fatal in the absence of early lung transplantation. Inheritance is X-linked dominant, and the disorder is often associated with perinatal and neonatal lethality in males. \n\n## **Multisystem Smooth Muscle Dysfunction Syndrome** \n\nHeterozygous pathogenic variants in _ACTA2_ , which encodes the protein **a** -actin, result in **multisystem smooth muscle dysfunction syndrome** . Manifestations can include chILD and pulmonary hypertension, together with congenital fixed mydriasis, aneurysmal patent ductus arteriosus, aortic and other arterial dilatations and aneurysms, cerebrovascular disease, intestinal hypoperistalsis, intestinal malrotation, and hypotonic bladder. \n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0576_13.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 24.9** Pathogenic variants in the X-linked gene Filamin A (FLNA) in a 9-month-old female who presented with pulmonary hypertension. (A) Axial lung window CT image shows bilateral multilobar hyperinflation. (B) Coronal brain MR image demonstrates nodular periventricular gray matter heterotopia. (From Semple T, Winant AJ, Lee EY. Childhood interstitial lung disease: imaging guidelines and recommendations. _Radiol Clin North Am._ 2022;60:83-111 [Fig. 13, p. 99].)\n\n<!-- p.576 -->\n\n## **_TBX4_ -Related Disorders** \n\nT-box transcription factor 4 is a crucial regulator of lung branching during embryogenesis, controlling the expression and activation of fibroblast growth factor 10. _TBX4_ is located at chromosomal locus 17q23.2. Pathogenic variants and deletions affecting _TBX4_ are associated with diverse phenotypes that can present from the neonatal period through adulthood. Pulmonary arterial hypertension is a common feature; additional findings include skeletal anomalies (e.g., **small patella syndrome** ), cardiac defects, and neurodevelopmental abnormalities. A variety of ILD phenotypes are seen, ranging from lethal diseases of neonatal lung development, acinar dysplasia, and congenital alveolar dysplasia to milder forms of lung growth abnormalities. \n\n## **Aminoacyl-tRNA SynthetaseRelated Disorders** \n\nPathogenic variants in genes encoding the aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase (ARS) family of enzymes, which regulate protein synthesis, are associated with multisystem pathology that includes ILD. Pathogenic variants in _IARS_ result in hypotonia and liver disease, along with ILDs such as alveolar proteinosis. Pathogenic variants in _MARS_ are associated with neurological abnormalities, liver disorders, and alveolar proteinosis. Pathogenic variants in _FARSA_ and _FARSB_ are associated with neurodevelopmental disorders such as diffuse brain calcifications with neurodegeneration, with hepatic involvement and ILD that can include subpleural cystic changes and bullous emphysema (Fig. 24.2G). \n\n## **Lysosomal Storage Diseases** \n\n**Niemann–Pick disease (NPD)** is a heterogeneous group of lysosomal storage disorders inherited in an autosomal recessive manner (see Chapter 46). Type A and type B result from an inherited deficiency of acid sphingomyelinase due to pathogenic variants in _SMPD1_ . Type A usually presents with neurological manifestations leading to death in infancy. Type B is the most frequent type presenting with visceral enlargement and survival into adulthood. Type C is due to defective cholesterol transport caused by pathogenic variants in _NPC1_ or _NPC2_ and is characterized by hepatosplenomegaly, ataxia, gaze palsies, hypotonia, frequent pulmonary infections, and severe liver disease. In NPD type C2, lung disease usually consists of alveolar proteinosis. Representative histopathology is depicted in Figure 24.8A–B. Symptoms of NPD are due to the accumulation of lipid-laden macrophages in the liver, spleen, bone marrow, brain, and lungs. Lung involvement is more frequent in type B and manifestations range from asymptomatic to severe respiratory failure. Respiratory symptoms may precede NPD diagnosis or may develop afterward. \n\n**Gaucher disease** is an autosomal recessive multisystemic sphingolipidosis characterized by organomegaly, hematological abnormalities, and skeletal involvement resulting \n\nfrom deficient activity of the lysosomal enzyme glucocerebrosidase, which is encoded by _GBA1_ (see Chapter 46). Lung involvement is uncommon at presentation, although it is seen in severe forms of the disease. Respiratory problems result from the interstitial infiltration by lipid-laden macrophages _(Gaucher cells)_ , but hepatopulmonary syndrome, as a complication of liver disease, can also be present. \n\n## **FINCA Syndrome** \n\nFibrosis, neurodegeneration, and cerebral angiomatosis (FINCA) is a rare autosomal recessive multiorgan disease caused by bi-allelic pathogenic variants in _NHLRC2_ . Key features include cerebral atrophy, hypoplastic corpus callosum, movement disorders, global developmental delays, intestinal malabsorption, growth delays, hemolytic anemia, and recurrent infections. Some affected individuals die early in infancy, while others may survive until childhood. ILD is prominent in infancy but may remain stable over time. Findings consist of interstitial fibrosis and granulomatous lesions. \n\n## **Pulmonary Alveolar Microlithiasis** \n\nPulmonary alveolar microlithiasis (PAM) is an ultra-rare autosomal recessive respiratory disease caused by pathogenic variants in _SLC34A2_ and characterized by lamellar deposition of calcium phosphate microliths within the alveoli. _SLC34A2_ encodes for a phosphate transporter in type II alveolar cells; pathology is related to defects in the removal of degraded phosphate. Manifestations at disease onset range from asymptomatic incidental imaging findings to nonproductive cough, exertional dyspnea, pneumothorax, and digital clubbing. Chest radiography in PAM shows a “sandstorm” appearance (Fig. 24.10), while HRCT shows micronodular calcified densities with thickening of the septa (Fig. 24.5). The differential diagnosis includes tuberculosis, sarcoidosis, histoplasmosis, pneumonic varicella, and pulmonary hemorrhage. Evidence-based treatment guidelines are lacking, though trialed modalities include whole lung lavage, glucocorticoids, and bisphosphonates. Lung transplantation may be considered in severe disease. \n\n## **Pulmonary Alveolar Proteinosis** \n\nPulmonary alveolar proteinosis (PAP) is characterized by surfactant accumulation in the alveoli, which leads to hypoxia and respiratory failure (see Chapter 26). PAP is related to decreased clearance of surfactant rather than increased production and can be classified as autoimmune, congenital, or secondary. **Autoimmune PAP** accounts for 90% of identified cases and is caused by the presence of antibodies directed against granulocyte macrophage colony stimulating factor (anti-GM-CSF antibodies), which decreases the functional macrophages responsible for surfactant clearance. No inciting triggers for the formation of\n\n<!-- p.577 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0578_01.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 24.10** Chest radiograph of a 25-year-old male patient with pulmonary alveolar microlithiasis, showing diffuse opacification in both lungs with a dense micronodular (“sandstorm”) appearance.  (From Ferreira Francisco FA, Pereira e Silva JL, Hochhegger B, Zanetti G, Marchiori E. Pulmonary alveolar microlithiasis. State-of-the-art review. _Respir Med._ 2013;107[1]:1-9, Elsevier.) \n\nanti-GM-CSF antibodies have been identified. **Congenital PAP** is caused by pathogenic variants affecting the GMCSF pathway or the regulation of surfactant levels. Genes encoding GM-CSF receptor subunits and implicated in congenital PAP include _CSF2RA_ and _CSF2RB_ . Surfactantrelated genes implicated in congenital PAP include _SFTPB_ , _SFTPC_ , _ABCA3_ , and _NKX2-1_ ; additional genes associated with congenital PAP include _SLC7A7_ , which is associated with **lysinuric protein intolerance** (see Chapters 26 and 43). **Secondary PAP** is caused by conditions that reduce the number or function of alveolar macrophages, such as myelodysplastic syndrome, hematological malignancy, lung cancer, mesothelioma, glioblastoma, chronic inflammatory syndromes, immune deficiencies, or pathogenic _GATA2_ variants affecting the number or function of mononuclear phagocytes. Environmental exposure to silica, talc, and other occupational compounds has been associated with secondary PAP as well. \n\nDiagnosis is suggested by HRCT findings and is supported by the presence of clinical symptoms consistent with PAP. Symptoms are nonspecific and approximately a third of affected individuals are asymptomatic. The most common manifestation is dyspnea, followed by cough. The presence of chest pain or hemoptysis usually suggests complications such as infection. Abnormalities most commonly observed on HRCT include ground-glass opacities, septal reticulations, and consolidations. Reticulations superimposed on ground glass form the _crazy-paving pattern_ characteristic of PAP \n\n(Figs. 24.2E and 24.4). Diagnosis is typically confirmed via the presence of a PAS-positive milky fluid on BAL, which indicates the presence of extracellular proteinaceous material. Biopsy is rarely needed to confirm diagnosis. The presence of serum anti-GM-CSF antibodies is consistent with a diagnosis of autoimmune PAP. \n\nTherapeutic management depends on cause and severity. Since there have been some cases of spontaneous regression, a watchful waiting approach may be appropriate in asymptomatic individuals. The treatment of choice is generally whole lung lavage, which eliminates the proteinaceous material through instillation of saline into the lung. Some studies have demonstrated the efficacy of subcutaneous and inhaled GM-CSF supplementation in autoimmune PAP. Additional strategies for patients who are resistant to other treatments include plasmapheresis and rituximab. The use of corticosteroids and other immunosuppressant treatments has been shown to be ineffective and to lead to increased complications. Lung transplantation is indicated in the setting of progressive treatment-resistant disease associated with respiratory failure. \n\n## **Hypersensitivity Pneumonitis** \n\nHypersensitivity pneumonitis (HP), also called **exogenous (extrinsic) allergic alveolitis** , is an ILD caused by IgGmediated inflammation triggered by inhaled allergens. Pediatric HP is often underappreciated given how less frequent it is in children relative to adults, though early diagnosis, removal of the suspected offending exposure, and initiation of treatment are essential to resolve induced lung damage and prevent disease progression. Delayed diagnosis is associated with development of a chronic and progressive form of HP that can lead to pulmonary fibrosis and even death. _HP is identified as the cause of chILD in up to 50% of children over the age of 2 years and should be considered in the differential diagnosis of chILD in almost all children older than 2 years_ (Fig. 24.2F). The most frequent antigens are from exposure to birds, rodents, fungi, bacteria, and low molecular weight chemical compounds. Treatment includes allergen elimination and antiinflammatory drugs, usually systemic corticosteroids with or without immunomodulators. \n\n## **Sarcoidosis** \n\nSarcoidosis is a multisystem inflammatory disorder of unknown etiology, though genetic susceptibility and environmental exposures are presumed to be contributory. The hallmark of sarcoidosis is the presence of noncaseating granulomas in affected tissues; pulmonary involvement consists of interstitial lung disease with reticular opacities and bilateral hilar adenopathy. Additional frequently affected tissues include the skin, eyes, and joints; other tissues include the heart, kidneys, central nervous system, the reticuloendothelial system, and muscles. Sarcoidosis is about 10 times more frequent in adults than in children. Symptoms are variable\n\n<!-- p.578 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0579_01.jpg)\n\n\nand depend on which organs or tissues are affected, though common respiratory features include a persistent dry cough and dyspnea. Other common findings include arthritis, erythema nodosum, fatigue, blurred vision (uveitis), neuropsychiatric dysfunction, and lymphadenopathy. \n\nDemonstration of noncaseating granulomas in the absence of fungi or mycobacteria on biopsy confirms diagnosis; alternative diagnoses that may cause granulomatous inflammation such as immunodeficiency, Blau syndrome, tuberculosis or other mycobacterial disease, eosinophilic granuloma, Crohn disease, or more rarely tumors and drug-induced granulomatosis should also be excluded. Transbronchial biopsy approaches are typically high yield, though mediastinal approaches may be required to access impacted lymph nodes for sampling. Tests such as serum angiotensin-converting enzyme levels can offer supporting evidence, as can elevated inflammatory markers and serum calcium levels. Additional testing should be obtained to assess for end-organ dysfunction, such as renal insufficiency or hepatic inflammation or dysfunction. Staging is based on chest imaging findings: stage I is characterized by bilateral hilar adenopathy; stage II consists of bilateral hilar adenopathy and reticular opacities; stage III features include reticular opacities with shrinking hilar nods; and stage IV is characterized by reticular opacities with fibrosis (Fig. 24.11). \n\nManagement requires a multidisciplinary approach and is based on intermittent courses of corticosteroids; steroid-sparing regimens may be considered in those unable to tolerate steroids. Most individuals improve with treatment, but relapses are common, and long-term management is usually required. \n\n## **Langerhans Cell Histiocytosis** \n\nLangerhans cell histiocytosis (LCH) is a rare clonal disorder characterized by the abnormal proliferation of Langerhans antigen-presenting cells and the accumulation of inflammatory granulomatous lesions with predominantly eosinophilic infiltration in target tissues (see Chapter 59). The exact cause is unknown, though pathogenic variants in _BRAF_ are identified in about 50% of affected individuals. LCH is more common in children than in adults. Disease can be disseminated or isolated to one or only several organ systems. Rash is the common presenting finding in children and typically consists of a scaly papules, nodules, or plaques that may mimic seborrheic dermatitis, though petechiae, bloody crusting, and firm induration distinguish the rash of LCH from seborrheic dermatitis. Most affected individuals have bone lesions that are often incidentally noted on imaging obtained for other indications, though lesions may at times \n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0579_07.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 24.11** _Scadding’s radiographic stages of_ sarcoidosis _with_ CT _correlation_ . (A and B) “Stage 1.” A 30-year-old man with sarcoidosis: (A) chest radiograph obtained during routine evaluation shows bilateral hilar, bilateral paratracheal, and aorticopulmonary window adenopathy _(arrows)_ , but no parenchymal findings and (B) CT chest shows bilateral hilar _(arrows)_ and subcarinal _(asterisk)_ adenopathy. (C and D) “Stage 2.” A 35-year-old man with sarcoidosis. (C) Chest radiograph shows upper lobe– predominant reticulonodular opacities and bilateral hilar and mediastinal lymphadenopathy. (D) Chest CT shows reticular opacities, ground-glass opacity, and mild architectural distortion in the upper lobes. Perilymphatic nodules cluster along the right major fissure and along peripheral septal lymphatics _(arrows)_ . \n\n_(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.579 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0580_01.jpg)\n\n\n**• Fig. 24.11, cont’d** (E and F) “Stage 3.” A 43-year-old woman with sarcoidosis: (E) chest radiograph shows upper lobe–predominant linear and bronchocentric nodular opacities and right lower lobe bronchocentric consolidation without definite hilar or mediastinal nodal enlargement and (F) CT shows confluent bronchocentric nodules in the posterior upper lobes. (G and H) “Stage 4.” A 37-year-old woman with severe fibrosis in the setting of sarcoidosis. (G) Radiograph shows severe bilateral upper lobe volume loss and traction bronchiectasis. Although the central pulmonary arteries are enlarged, mediastinal and hilar adenopathy is not present. (H) Coronal reformatted minimum-intensity projection (minIP) CT image shows varicoid traction bronchiectasis in the upper lobes, with severe upward hilar retraction. The bronchi are retracted centrally, indicating fibrosis most severe along the bronchovascular lymphatics. Large apical bullae are due to parenchymal distortion and destruction from severe fibrosis.  (From Little BP. Sarcoidosis: overview of pulmonary manifestations and imaging. _Semin Roentgenol_ . 2015;50[1]:52-64, Elsevier.) \n\nbe painful. Nodal involvement is common as well, present in up to 30% of affected individuals. Cystic and interstitial lung disease is found in up to 20% of affected individuals. **Pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis** is an entity found almost exclusively in current or former tobacco smokers, typically presenting in the third decade of life. Findings consist of interstitial lung disease with associated symptoms of cough and dyspnea; rarely, individuals with pulmonary LCH will have extrapulmonary manifestations as well. \n\nTreatment for LCH usually consists of chemotherapy with or without radiation therapy, depending on the type of organ involvement and severity. Some individuals with the _BRAF_<sup>V600E</sup> variant have been reported to receive targeted gene therapies such as vemurafenib. Management of pulmonary LCH form includes smoking cessation habit and, in progressive disease, prednisone, methotrexate, and chemotherapy (cytarabine, cladribine, vinblastine) have been used. \n"});
